        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"unmet\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"The supplied text is a narrative review that explicitly addresses the status of clinical trials for microbiome interventions in cancer. It states that as of late 2025, 'no completed Phase III randomized trials have yet confirmed a reproducible survival benefit of microbiome-based interventions in oncology.' The authors note that current evidence relies on Phase I/II trials (e.g., FMT in melanoma, engineered bacteria like SYNB1891 and MRx0518) and meta-analyses of these smaller studies. The text details specific ongoing or completed early-phase trials (e.g., NCT03353402, NCT04167137) but confirms they are not Phase III. It also highlights the lack of published primary outcome data on overall survival (OS) or progression-free survival (PFS) from large-scale randomized settings, identifying this as a critical gap in the field.\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"The text provides detailed mechanistic insights into how microbial metabolites (SCFAs, tryptophan derivatives, bile acids) modulate immunity, which serves as the biological rationale for why Phase III trials are being pursued. It lists specific interventions currently in development, such as Fecal Microbiota Transplantation (FMT), defined bacterial consortia (SER-401), and engineered live biotherapeutics (SYNB1891, MRx0518), along with their current trial phases (mostly Phase I or Ib). It also discusses regulatory challenges (FDA/EMA guidelines for Live Biotherapeutic Products) and safety concerns (e.g., transmission of multidrug-resistant organisms via FMT) that complicate the design and execution of large-scale trials.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"The supplied material does not identify any completed large-scale Phase III randomized controlled trial (RCT) of microbiome interventions in ICI-treated solid tumor patients with published primary efficacy outcomes (OS/PFS/ORR). Instead, it explicitly confirms the absence of such studies, stating that the field currently lacks Phase III confirmation. Therefore, the success criterion of identifying a completed Phase III RCT with published primary outcome data is not met by this source; the source only confirms the gap exists.\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"Use this review to definitively state that no completed Phase III RCTs meeting the criteria currently exist in the literature (as of the review's publication). Cite the review's summary of the current evidence landscape (reliance on Phase I/II and meta-analyses) to contextualize the gap. Utilize the detailed descriptions of ongoing Phase I/II trials (e.g., FMT, engineered bacteria) and the discussed mechanistic rationales to outline what future Phase III trials might look like or why they are challenging to conduct, but clearly distinguish these from the requested completed Phase III evidence.\"\n}",
